verdicts.corrected_fix:
Minimal fix, which is all the demonstrated bug needs:
- In c08/analysis.py:477, change `except (TypeError, ValueError):` to `except (TypeError, ValueError, OverflowError):`.
- In tests/test_c08.py, add `[1e400, 1]` and `[2015, -1e400]` to the bad-input loop in test_daily_api_withholds_the_p04_test_windows_and_rejects_bad_months. Python parses 1e400 as float('inf'), and the test client sends it as Infinity. Both must return 400 with a JSON body.

For consistency, add OverflowError to the older except at c08/app.py:34 (`except (TypeError, ValueError, IndexError, OverflowError)`). That gap is outside this change but is the same bug.

Do not add [2015.5, 1] to the test unless you choose the optional strict check. If you want whole numbers only, use a helper that keeps int() inside the try and accepts whole-number floats such as 2015.0, which Python clients send:

    def _whole(v):
        if isinstance(v, float) and v.is_integer():
            v = int(v)
        if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, (int, str)):
            raise ValueError(v)
        return int(v)

Call it as `year, month = _whole(year), _whole(month)` inside the same `except (TypeError, ValueError, OverflowError)`. Do not use str.isdigit(): it accepts '²', which int() rejects. Checked on sample values: 2015, 2015.0 and '2015' are accepted; 2015.9, inf, -inf, nan, True, None, [1], '²' and '2015.5' are rejected.

Keep the reviewer's approach. Define the two flags it uses, and stop the hover cue on a button that is unavailable.

In c08/web/app.js renderDaily, replace lines 424-425 with:

  const atStart = y === META.years[0] && mo === 1, atEnd = y === META.years[1] && mo === 12;
  $("daily-prev").setAttribute("aria-disabled", String(atStart));
  $("daily-next").setAttribute("aria-disabled", String(atEnd));

The buttons stay focusable. stepDaily's range check at line 413 remains what actually stops the move, and in the browser it already blocks any request before 2003 or after 2025.

In c08/web/style.css, after the .daily-nav rules, add:

  .daily-nav button[aria-disabled="true"] { opacity: .45; cursor: default; }
  .daily-nav button[aria-disabled="true"]:hover { border-color: var(--axis); }

The second rule is needed because `button:hover` at line 70 would otherwise still darken the border.

If the native disabled attribute is preferred instead, two changes are needed:
- Move focus before disabling: `if (document.activeElement === btn) $("daily-month").focus();`
- Add a `.daily-nav button:disabled` rule with the same opacity and cursor, because the author rule at style.css:68 hides the browser's disabled look.

1. Map a network failure inside post(), around the fetch() call only. This works in every browser: run()'s `includes("Failed to fetch")` check matches only Chromium's wording. It also fixes doExport, which shows the raw message as well:
```js
async function post(url, body, signal) {
  let r;
  try {
    r = await fetch(url, { method: "POST", headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" }, body: JSON.stringify(body), signal });
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.name === "AbortError") throw e;
    throw new Error("The local server is not responding. Start it with: python -m c08.app");
  }
  if (!r.ok) { /* unchanged */ }
  return r;
}
```
Leave loadDaily's `m.textContent = e.message` as it is. Rendering errors then keep their own text.

2. Keep the controls and the content in step:
   - Take the two `disabled` lines out of renderDaily and put them in a helper that reads the selects: `function dailyNav(){ const y=+$("daily-year").value, mo=+$("daily-month").value; $("daily-prev").disabled = y===META.years[0]&&mo===1; $("daily-next").disabled = y===META.years[1]&&mo===12; }`.
   - Call dailyNav() at the top of loadDaily.
   - In loadDaily's catch, for the newest request that was not aborted, add: `DAILY = null; $("daily").innerHTML = ""; $("daily-notes").innerHTML = ""; $("daily-title").textContent = "Day by day";`.
   - Do not rely on putting the selects back to DAILY.month.

3. On a theme change, redraw from the cached reply instead of fetching again:
   - Give render() a parameter: `function render(refetchDaily = true) { … if (refetchDaily) loadDaily(); else if (DAILY) renderDaily(); … }`.
   - Change the listener to `() => RESULT && render(false)`.
   - If a cache test is still wanted, compare `JSON.stringify(dailyBody())` saved with DAILY, not DAILY.region.

In c08/web/style.css, keep part 1, drop the 150 px width and pad by the sticky width plus one day column:

```css
table.daily thead th:first-child { position: sticky; left: 0; z-index: 2; background: var(--surface); }
table.daily th[scope="row"] { width: 96px; min-width: 96px; }  /* replaces max-width: 150px, which table cells ignore */
/* Sticky column (96 px) plus one 30 px day column. Chrome does not scroll a focused link that is already
   partly visible, so the padding must leave room for a whole link and its focus ring beside the column. */
#daily { scroll-padding-left: 126px; }
```

This does not depend on column widths: in renderDaily() (c08/web/app.js), next to the mousemove listener, add:

```js
tbl.addEventListener("focusin", (evt) => {
  const th = evt.target.closest("thead th");
  if (!th) return;
  const edge = tbl.querySelector("thead th").getBoundingClientRect().right;  // the sticky corner's right edge
  const left = th.getBoundingClientRect().left;
  if (left < edge) $("daily").scrollLeft -= edge - left;
});
```

Use it together with the sticky-corner rule. Both versions measured 0 of 29-30 Shift+Tab stops with the focused link or its counts hidden at every width tested.
